From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Wed, 28 Sep 1994 16:43:14 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-579] Penrose's "Shadows of the Mind"

[better late than never...]

Paul Cisek wrote on Sept 20 1994,
>>From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
>>X-Message-Number: #94-9-320
>>
>>I did think his intro section where he laid out views A,B,C,D on AI
>>was well done - and illuminated issues I've seen clouded here.  I'd
>>have to call myself a B, and I thought his critique of A was worth
>>reading. 
>
>Could you summarize what these four different AI views were (if you can
>recall)?  Illumination is always good...

A.  All thinking is computation; conscious awareness is evoked by
    carrying out the right computation.

B.  Awareness is a feature of the brain's physical activity; it can be
    simulated on a computer, but this need not evoke awareness.

C.  Physical activity evokes awareness, and it can't be simulated on a
    computer. 

D.  Awareness can't be explained by physics, computation, or any
    scientific terms.

Much of his criticisms of A regarded ambiguities in when a computation
is "carried out".  

Robin Hanson

