From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Wed, 28 Sep 1994 18:24:15 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-9-581] Omega Point

A corollary of the credit theory of identity,  is that a resurrected
exact copy of me is _not_ me.  The proof is as follows: I have
no memory of a previous or parallel life.  Therefore an exact copy
of me would have no memory of a previous or parallel life.  If a copy
has no memory of a previous or parallel life, then it cannot give credit
to such a life.  In particular, a copy of my life cannot give credit 
to me.  Therefore by the credit theory, that copy is not me.

Thus resurrection does not constitute simply creating an exact
copy of me.  It constitutes creating a copy that starts up where
I left off, for example at death.  This entails:

* fixing the damage that caused the death, and the damage
caused by death
* either making an exact copy of my current environment
(a problem of gargantuan computational proportions), or
adapting me to an abruptly new environment.  Any
noticeable imperfection in continuity of the environment
automatically allows the copy to deduce the existence of a 
previous life and subsequent resurrection.

Another way to analyze it is as follows: assume that a copy
of me is made at the Omega Point, at each infinitesimal
stage of my life.  If an infinite number of such copies
can be made that also entail copies of my environment with
computationally intractable continuity error, then the chance
chances are practically 100% that I am one of those copies.
Interestingly, this has strong resonances with the "Many
Worlds" cosmology.

